WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.960
It's a genre of gunfights, fisticuffs, and
car chases, so fans are expecting excitement

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:06.860
— and fast.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:11.740
From costumed gangs gathering in the streets
to clandestine murders that’d chill the

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:17.160
spines of the coldest killers, these are some
of the greatest opening scenes in action movie

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:17.660
history.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:19.620
Meet the Warriors

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.410
In The Warriors, director Walter Hill wastes
no time in setting up the movie's stakes from

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:25.320
the very first frame.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.120
“Everybody says that Cyrus is the one-and-only.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.600
I think we better go out and look for ourselves.”

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:37.340
The way Hill drops exposition during this
intro is incredibly economical.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.200
A big meeting is about to go down, and we
get a view of a subway map to show us the

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.340
Warriors are about to find themselves far
from their Coney Island turf.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.690
And as our ragtag heroes plan their odyssey
to the Bronx, we're introduced to a wide and

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.620
unusual variety of gangs, all on similar journeys.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:55.440
In just six minutes, we've met our main characters,
we understand the stakes, and we've got a

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.819
feel for this fantasy world.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:00.719
So when the truce is broken and things get
crazy, we're already primed for what's going

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:01.220
down.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:02.840
"Can you dig it?"

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:05.060
Tomb raiding

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.640
It could easily be argued that Raiders of
the Lost Ark features the greatest opening

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:10.900
sequence of all time.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.660
After the Paramount logo dissolves into an
actual mountain — which is a nice enough

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:19.270
touch in itself — Spielberg plunges viewers
into a veritable love letter to old-timey

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:21.380
serials and Saturday morning adventures.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.300
And at the centre of it all is that first
reveal of the movie’s enigmatic hero.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.140
But it's when Jones steps into that hidden
temple that you really know Raiders is going

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:34.900
to be special.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:39.439
He’s faced with one setback after another,
including that iconic rolling boulder, and

00:01:39.439 --> 00:01:43.549
even when Jones makes his way outside, he
finds himself face to face with his arch-rival

00:01:43.549 --> 00:01:46.049
— and an army of very unhappy natives.

00:01:46.049 --> 00:01:49.939
And while this classic opening is non-stop
action, it teaches us everything we need to

00:01:49.939 --> 00:01:50.939
know about Jones.

00:01:50.939 --> 00:01:52.710
He's a man of action and a problem solver.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:56.210
He's a little cocky, and every so often, he
screws up royally.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:02:02.160
Most importantly, this guy can face arrows,
arachnids, pistols and deep, dark pits, and

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:03.900
nothing at all can faze him…

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.580
well, almost nothing.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.060
“There’s a big snake in the plane, Jock!”

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:14.800
Kicking off

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:20.319
It's easy to forget nowadays, but when The
Matrix was released in 1999, it quickly became

00:02:20.319 --> 00:02:22.430
a cultural force to be reckoned with.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:23.340
And it all began with a kick.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:27.580
The movie opens with a wall of digital rain
and a mysterious conversation between two

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.920
unseen people about somebody called "The One."

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.520
“Morpheus believes he is the One."

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:33.620
"Do you?"

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:35.460
"It doesn't matter what I believe."

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:37.200
"You don’t, do you?"

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:41.080
That's when the cops show up, moving down
an abandoned apartment hallway and busting

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:41.860
down a door.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:46.280
On the other side sits Trinity, clad in shiny
black leather and ready for a showdown.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:49.660
We don't know why the police want her, but
we quickly realize they're never going to

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:50.220
catch her.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:00.700
Audiences were hooked from scene one, and
the Wachowskis owe a major debt to Carrie

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.560
Anne-Moss for kicking their film off in the
right way.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:05.320
A fresh Angle

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.120
Hot Fuzz works on many levels.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:14.280
It's a brilliant comedy, a slasher film, and
a fantastic satire of Michael Bay-style action

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:14.780
movies.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:19.420
But it's also a solid action flick that revels
in the joys of the genre it lampoons.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:19.920
Sgt.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.400
Nicholas Angel, on the other hand, absolutely
hates all that action movie foolishness.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:29.700
Played to serious perfection by Simon Pegg,
Angel has no time for fun and games.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:34.459
From his first appearance through to the ensuing
rapid-fire montage of Angel cleaning up the

00:03:34.459 --> 00:03:38.430
streets of London, it’s clear that the man
is all about bringing down bad guys properly…

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.360
with a few injuries along the way.

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:43.640
“And sustained three injuries in the line
of duty.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:48.340
Most recently in December, when wounded by
a man dressed as Father Christmas."

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:54.180
The whole opening montage is a fantastic setup
for the kicker, in which Angel is unceremoniously

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.580
kicked off the force…for being too perfect.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:00.420
“Well however you spin this, there's one
thing you haven't taken into account.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.840
And that's what the team is gonna make of
this.”

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:07.640
Death in Prague

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:12.640
A lot of fans were skeptical about the casting
of Daniel Craig as James Bond, so it was crucial

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.950
that Casino Royale made a good first impression.

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:19.280
It was also essential that the movie let audiences
know they were in store for something new.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.880
This was Bond in the age of Jason Bourne.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:23.620
The movie needed a killer opening.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.880
Shot in gloriously cold black and white, Casino
Royale’s opening tells the story of Bond’s

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:31.120
first two kills as an MI6 agent.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.860
We watch as he savagely destroys an enemy
spy, beating the dude to a pulp before drowning

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:37.050
him in a bathroom sink.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.080
“Made you feel it, did he?”

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:44.500
This vicious murder is intercut with a scene
set in Prague, in which Bond confronts a traitorous

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:45.260
section chief.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:49.230
Although he finds himself on the wrong end
of a gun, it soon becomes clear that he's

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.320
a spy who can think two steps ahead.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:55.060
“Well, you needn’t worry.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:57.600
The second is -”

